  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 940 PM MDT Thu May 21 2026\n\nLong-wave trough over the western US is finally drifting east at \nthis time. Southwest flow continues over the CWA within this long-\nwave, with one weak short-wave just now passing through the area, \nand the main low now cut off near the northern Baja. Residual \nmoisture at the surface hung around today and aided in isolated \nshowers and thunderstorms along our southern CWA this \nafternoon/early evening as that short-wave moved across. \n\nPost short-wave, low level moisture will be scoured out on Friday as \nwinds turn from south to southwest. Meanwhile, aloft, Baja upper low \nwill drift over the Sonoran and Chihuahuan Deserts of northern \nMexico before frittering away by Saturday. In it's place, weak \nridging will build over New Mexico. This pattern will last into \nSunday, leaving behind a dry Friday-weekend. \n\nNext Pacific low moving to near SoCal Sunday night to western \nArizona Monday will bring an increase in clouds. Low level \nmoisture will also increase with model dewpoints back into the \n40s, initially to areas west of the RG Valley Monday afternoon, \nand eventually to all areas Monday night. Expect some thunderstorms\nMon afternoon in the west, spreading eastward in the evening. \nUpper low lifts out toward the Four Corners Tuesday, scouring away\nmost of the moisture; however enough moisture remains in the far \neastern CWA to warrant a low POP for Tuesday. Temperatures for the\nforecast period will be above normal this weekend, but otherwise \nat seasonable levels.",
